from helpers import danger, caution, tip, check, table, fig

def part4(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p4"><small>Part 4</small>Making Bricks &amp; Blocks</h1>']
    h.append('<p>Bricks are the most forgiving masonry material you can make yourself: clay is almost everywhere, the tools are wood and fire, and a family can make thousands in a season. Sun-dried <b>adobe</b> needs no fuel; <b>fired brick</b> survives rain and frost; <b>compressed earth blocks</b> and <b>concrete blocks</b> are fast where you have a press or cement.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="clay">4.1 Finding and testing clay</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Look below the dark topsoil: road cuts, stream banks, pond bottoms, gullies, foundation and well spoil, old brickyard sites (common place names: “Brickyard Road,” “Kiln Creek”). Good brick clay is sticky when wet, hard when dry, and red, yellow, gray or brown. Glacial lake and river clays across the northern US and Canada, the red Piedmont clays of the Southeast and valley clays in the West all made bricks historically. Remove pebbles — especially <b>limestone pebbles</b>, which turn to quicklime in the kiln and later swell and burst the brick (“lime blowing”).</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.clay_tests(), "<b>Figure 5. Three field tests for clay.</b> Also do the jar test (Figure 2A): good brick earth is roughly 20–30% clay with the rest silt and sand; adobe soil 15–25% clay and 55–75% sand.", "max-width:70%"))
    h.append('<h2 id="temper">4.2 Preparing (tempering) the clay</h2>')
    h.append('<ol><li><b>Dig in autumn, weather over winter</b> if you can — frost and rain break lumps down. Otherwise soak it.</li>'
             '<li><b>Soak and break up</b>: soak dry lumps in a pit for 1–3 days, then tread barefoot or with animals, or hoe, until it is an even, plastic mass with no lumps (“pugging”). Pick out stones and roots.</li>'
             '<li><b>Adjust</b>: if the shrinkage bar cracked or shrank &gt; 8–10%, add sharp sand or <b>grog</b> (crushed fired brick/pottery) — start at 1 part to 4 parts clay. If the coil test breaks, the soil is too sandy — blend with a fatter clay.</li>'
             '<li><b>For adobe</b>: add chopped straw, grass or manure fiber (2–4 in long), about 1 part straw to 5 parts soil by volume. It reduces cracking and adds tensile strength. <b>Don’t</b> add straw to bricks for firing — it burns out and leaves voids (fine sawdust or coal dust can be used deliberately as internal fuel).</li>'
             '<li>Make 10–20 test bricks and dry and fire them before committing to thousands.</li></ol>')
    h.append('<h2 id="mold">4.3 Molding</h2>')
    h.append('<p><b>Sand-struck</b>: dip the mold in water, then in sand, so the brick releases cleanly. <b>Slop-molded</b>: just dip in water (softer clay, smoother brick). Throw a clot of clay slightly larger than the cell <i>hard</i> into the mold so it fills the corners, strike off the excess with a wet stick, and lift the mold straight up off the flat, sanded ground (“ground molding”) or tip it out onto a pallet board. An experienced pair can make 1,000–2,000 bricks a day by hand.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.brick_mold(), "<b>Figure 6. A wooden brick mold and the throwing sequence.</b> Hardwood planks ~1 in (25 mm) thick, joints screwed or dovetailed, inside faces planed smooth; steel strip on the top edges makes them last.", "max-width:66%"))
    h.append('<h2 id="dry">4.4 Drying</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Lay new bricks flat on level, sanded ground for 1–3 days until they can be handled; trim rough edges with a knife, then stand them on edge for even drying. After about a week, stack them in open lattice <b>hacks</b> (finger-gaps between bricks, crossing each course) under a roof or tarp so rain can’t reach them and wind can. Shade them from hot sun and drying wind for the first days — fast drying cracks bricks. Fired bricks need 2–4 weeks to be fully dry; adobes 3–4 weeks. <b>Test</b>: break one — the inside should be the same light color as the outside, with no cool damp core. Wet bricks waste fuel and explode in the kiln.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="adobe">4.5 Adobe vs fired brick</h2>')
    h.append(table(["", "Adobe (sun-dried)", "Fired brick"], [
        ["Fuel needed", "None", "A lot: ~1 ton of wood fires roughly 1,500–3,000 bricks in a small kiln"],
        ["Strength", "~300 psi (2 MPa)", "1,500–10,000+ psi"],
        ["Water / frost", "Dissolves in water; must have stone or concrete base, overhanging roof and lime or mud plaster", "Durable; hard-fired bricks resist frost"],
        ["Size", "10 × 14 × 4 in (25 × 35 × 10 cm), ~30–40 lb", "~8 × 4 × 2½ in, ~4–5 lb"],
        ["Mortar", "Mud of the same soil (with straw)", "Lime or cement-lime mortar"],
        ["Best climate", "Dry: Southwest, Great Basin, Mexico, highlands", "Anywhere; essential in wet and freezing climates"],
        ["Earthquakes", "Heavy and brittle — needs bond beams, buttresses, low walls", "Reinforced or confined masonry needed in seismic zones"]]))
    h.append('<h2 id="kilns">4.6 Kilns: clamp, scove and updraft</h2>')
    h.append('<div class="cols2"><div><h3>Clamp / scove kiln</h3><ol><li>Level and drain a site; lay a course of fired or dry bricks as a floor.</li><li>Build 2–4 fire tunnels (arched with corbelled green bricks) running through the base, 3–4 ft apart, each about 1–2 bricks wide and 3–4 courses high.</li><li>Set the green bricks above on edge in alternating directions, finger-width apart, in a block 10–20 ft square and 8–12 ft high (5,000–30,000 bricks). Smaller clamps (~1,000 bricks) work but waste more fuel.</li><li>Case the outside with 1–2 layers of old bricks and daub it 2–4 in thick with mud (scove). Leave the top open with loose bricks.</li><li>Fire as in 4.7 — slow for days, then hard. Plaster over any cracks where fire breaks through.</li></ol><p>Outer and top bricks will be underfired; re-fire them in the next clamp or use them inside.</p></div>'
             '<div><h3>Updraft kiln</h3><p>Permanent walls 1–2 bricks thick, 4–6 ft high, mortared with clay, around a chamber 6–10 ft square. Two or more arched fireboxes run under a floor of spaced bricks (or a grate of arches) that lets flame rise through the setting. The top is covered loosely with old brick or roofing iron so you can adjust the draft; bands of steel or timber around the outside stop the walls bulging. Better fuel economy and more even firing than a clamp; the bottom gets hottest, so set the strongest, densest bricks low.</p>'
             '<h3>Fuel</h3><p>Dry hardwood split small, softwood for fast heat, brush and corn cobs for the early stage, coal or charcoal if available. Plan on one to two times the weight of the bricks in wood for a small kiln (Practical Action reports 3–8 MJ per kg of brick for traditional clamps).</p></div></div>')
    h.append(fig(D.brick_kilns(), "<b>Figure 7. A clamp or scove kiln (left) and a small updraft kiln (right).</b> A clamp is built from the green bricks themselves and dismantled after firing; a scove kiln is a clamp with fireboxes at the base and an outer plaster of mud (the “scove”). An updraft kiln has permanent walls and is re-used.", "max-width:74%"))
    h.append('<h2 id="firing">4.7 Firing: temperatures and stages</h2>')
    h.append('<ul><li><b>Water-smoking</b> (1–3 days): a small fire at the tunnel mouths. White steam rises from the top. Rushing this makes bricks burst. Hold your hand over the top or a cold metal sheet: when it stops getting wet, move on.</li>'
             '<li><b>Heating</b>: increase the fire steadily over 1–3 days. Above dull red, keep plenty of air moving so the carbon in the clay burns out (otherwise bricks have black cores and bloat).</li>'
             '<li><b>Full fire / soak</b>: when bricks are bright orange through the spy-holes and those at the top are glowing red, hold that heat for 12–24 h. Shrinkage of the setting (it sinks by 1–2 in) is a good sign. If bricks start to glaze, slump or stick together, you’re over-firing — back off.</li>'
             '<li><b>Cooling</b>: close the fire mouths and top with mud and bricks and let it cool slowly — 2–7 days for a small kiln. Cold air on hot bricks cracks them.</li></ul>')
    h.append(fig(D.firing_curve(), "<b>Figure 8. Firing stages for clay bricks.</b> Clay must reach roughly 900–1,100 °C (depending on the clay) for the particles to fuse into ceramic (Practical Action: <i>Ten Rules for Energy Efficient, Cost Effective Brick Firing</i>).", "max-width:68%"))
    h.append('<h2 id="testbrick">4.8 Testing fired bricks</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Test", "Good brick", "Underfired / poor"], [
        ["Strike two bricks together", "Clear metallic ring", "Dull thud"],
        ["Scratch with a fingernail / steel nail", "No mark from fingernail; nail barely scratches", "Scratches easily, dusty"],
        ["Break one", "Even color through, no black core, fine texture, no lime lumps", "Black or soft core, white specks (lime), voids"],
        ["Drop from shoulder height onto another brick", "Doesn’t break (corners may chip)", "Breaks"],
        ["Soak 24 h, weigh before/after", "Gains &lt; 15% of dry weight (frost-resistant); &lt; 20% OK under cover", "&gt; 20–25%: interior use or re-fire"],
        ["Soak several days, then rub", "Unchanged", "Softens, crumbles, swells (re-fire it)"]]))
    h.append(tip("Stop lime blowing", "If your clay had any limestone grit, dunk fired bricks in water for a few minutes (“docking”) straight after they come out of the kiln. Any lime particles slake while the brick is still unused instead of popping chunks out of a finished wall."))
    h.append('<h2 id="ceb">4.9 Compressed earth blocks (CEB)</h2>')
    h.append('<p>A compressed earth block is damp sandy soil squeezed hard in a press — originally the hand-lever <b>CINVA-Ram</b> (Colombia, 1950s); now many manual and powered presses. No firing, little water, and very regular blocks. Use soil with 10–25% clay and plenty of sand, screened to ¼ in (6 mm). Add a <b>stabilizer</b>: 5–10% portland cement by weight for sandy soils, or 6–10% hydrated lime for clayey soils (Auroville Earth Institute). Mix dry, then add water until a squeezed handful holds its shape and breaks cleanly in two when dropped from waist height. Press at once (cement starts setting in under an hour), stack in the shade and keep damp under plastic for 2–4 weeks. Typical dry strength 400–1,000 psi (3–7 MPa). Without a press you can build a simple lever press from heavy timber and steel plate; a 20:1 lever with one adult’s weight on the end gives roughly 1–2 tons of force — enough for a usable block.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="cmu">4.10 Concrete blocks by hand</h2>')
    h.append('<p>A 94-lb bag of cement with 6–8 parts sand/fine gravel makes roughly 15–25 hollow blocks. Make blocks on a smooth pallet board, tamp in thin layers, strike off, lift the mold and set the block aside without touching it. Cover and keep damp for at least a week, and don’t lay blocks until they are 2–4 weeks old. Test: a good 2-week block dropped flat from knee height onto grass shouldn’t break.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.block_mold(), "<b>Figure 9. Hand mold for standard 8 × 8 × 16 in hollow concrete blocks.</b> Lumber or steel; the cores leave cells for rebar and grout. A solid version (no cores) is easier: use a wetter 1:2:3 mix, leave in the mold overnight, and strip the next day.", "max-width:68%"))
    h.append('<h2 id="laying">4.11 Laying brick and block</h2>')
    h.append(fig(D.brick_bonds(), "<b>Figure 10. The four classic brick bonds</b> (after Brick Industry Association Technical Note 30). Thicker walls of two or more wythes need headers or metal ties to act as one wall.", "max-width:68%"))
    h.append(check("Bricklaying basics", [
        "Start on a level, cured footing. Lay the corners (“leads”) 4–6 courses high first, checked with level and plumb line; then stretch a string between the corners for each course",
        "Wet fired bricks and blocks lightly before laying in hot weather (damp, not dripping) so they don’t suck water from the mortar; lay adobe in mud mortar of the same soil",
        "Mortar joints ⅜ in (10 mm). Butter the end of each brick; fill joints fully — empty head joints leak and weaken the wall",
        "Overlap every vertical joint by at least ¼ of a brick, ideally ½; never stack joints in line",
        "Tool the joints (concave or V) when the mortar is thumbprint-hard — sheds water and compacts the surface",
        "Mortar: lime 1:3 for soft brick and stone; Type N cement-lime 1:1:6 for general work; Type S 1:½:4½ below grade. Use within ~2 hours (cement) — don’t re-temper old cement mortar more than once",
        "Mist and cover new work for 3 days; protect from frost (don’t lay below ~40 °F / 5 °C without protection) and from rain on the top course",
        "Build no more than about 4–5 ft (1.2–1.5 m) of height per day with lime mortar so lower courses can stiffen"]))
    return "\n".join(h)

def part5(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p5"><small>Part 5</small>Building Without Cement: Earth &amp; Stone</h1>']
    h.append('<p>If you have no binder at all, you can still build durable, thick-walled structures. Earth walls are cheap, fire-proof, good thermal mass and quiet. Their enemies are water and earthquakes: every earth technique needs <b>“a good hat and good boots”</b> — wide roof overhangs and a stone or concrete base that lifts the earth at least 12 in (300 mm) above the ground and splash zone.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Method", "What it is", "Soil", "Wall thickness", "Speed / labor", "Best where"], [
        ["<b>Rammed earth</b>", "Damp soil compacted in layers inside forms", "Sandy, 10–20% clay (≈70% sand+gravel)", "12–24 in (18 in typical)", "Heavy labor, fast walls with crew", "Dry to moderate climates; stabilize with 5–10% cement in wet ones"],
        ["<b>Cob</b>", "Wet earth + straw stacked by hand, no forms", "Clayey subsoil + sand + lots of straw", "18–24 in", "Slow (12–18 in of height a day), simple", "Mild, wet climates (UK, PNW); sculptural"],
        ["<b>Adobe</b>", "Sun-dried molded bricks in mud mortar", "15–25% clay, sandy, with straw", "10–14 in+", "Molding season, then quick", "Arid Southwest, Mexico"],
        ["<b>Earthbags</b>", "Woven poly bags filled with damp soil, tamped, with barbed wire between courses", "Almost any soil; gravel bags at base", "15–18 in", "Fast, few tools", "Anywhere; domes, root cellars, shelters"],
        ["<b>Dry stone</b>", "Stone fitted without mortar", "—", "Battered, 2 ft+ at base", "Skilled, slow", "Fieldstone regions: Northeast, Midwest, mountains"],
        ["<b>Soil-cement</b>", "Soil + 5–12% cement, compacted", "Sandy soils", "Floors 4–6 in; walls as rammed earth", "Moderate", "Floors, paths, stabilized walls, footings for light buildings"]]))
    h.append('<h2 id="rammed">5.1 Rammed earth</h2>')
    h.append(fig(D.rammed_earth_form(), "<b>Figure 11. Rammed-earth formwork.</b> Forms are moved up (or along) as each section is finished. Corners and openings need their own form ends (“stop ends”). After USDA Farmers’ Bulletin 1500 and SDSU Agricultural Experiment Station Circular 149.", "max-width:77%"))
    h.append('<p><b>Soil</b>: ideally about 70–75% sand and fine gravel and 25–30% silt plus clay, with no organic matter. Too much clay shrinks and cracks (SDSU tests found walls with over 35–40% colloids performed poorly). <b>Moisture</b>: squeeze a handful — it should hold together when squeezed and shatter when dropped from waist height (usually 8–12% water). Too wet and it won’t compact; too dry and it won’t bind. Fill 6–8 in of loose soil, ram with overlapping blows from the edges to the center until the tamper rings, then add the next lift. Strip forms immediately; the wall can carry itself at once. Cut window and door openings with timber or concrete lintels built in as you go. Plaster with lime (not cement, which traps moisture) or leave exposed under deep eaves.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="cob">5.2 Cob and adobe</h2>')
    h.append('<p><b>Cob</b>: mix clayey subsoil with sand (often about 1 part clay soil to 1–2 parts sand) and plenty of long straw by treading on a tarp; stack lumps (“cobs”) on the stone base, kneading each into the layer below with fingers or a stick. Build 12–18 in a day and let it firm up; trim the faces flat with a spade when leather-hard. Set wooden lintels and window frames into the wall as it rises. <b>Adobe</b>: see Part 4 for molding. Lay in mud mortar of the same soil, overlapping joints by half a brick; build bond beams at the top (Part 8). The New Mexico Earthen Building Materials Code (14.7.4 NMAC) is a practical reference for adobe, rammed earth and CEB: it limits wall heights and stories, and requires a minimum unit strength of 300 psi (and 18 in minimum thickness for rammed-earth exterior walls).</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="earthbag">5.3 Earthbags</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Fill woven polypropylene bags (feed, sand or rice bags; or long “tube” bags) with damp subsoil, lay them in running bond, tamp each course flat with a hand tamper, and lay <b>two strands of 4-point barbed wire</b> on top of every course as “Velcro” mortar and tensile reinforcement. Use gravel-filled bags for the bottom 2–3 courses as a drainage break. Polypropylene breaks down in sunlight within months — plaster or cover the bags quickly (lime or earth plaster over chicken wire or directly onto the weave). Earthbag domes, vaults and buried shelters work even without timber for a roof; straight walls need buttresses or curves every 10–12 ft and a bond beam.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="stone">5.4 Dry stone and stone with mud mortar</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Build on a trench of rubble below the topsoil. Lay the biggest stones low, long axis <b>into</b> the wall; batter (lean) both faces inward about 1 in per foot of height; place a <b>through-stone</b> spanning the whole wall every 3 ft along and every 2 ft up; fill the core tightly with small stones (“hearting”), never dirt; cross every joint (one over two, two over one). A dry-stone wall 4–5 ft high needs about 2 ft width at the base. For buildings, lay stone in clay or lime mortar and keep walls thick (18–24 in) and low.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="soilcement">5.5 Soil-cement and stabilized earth</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Even a little cement turns sandy soil into a durable material. Mix 1 part cement to 8–12 parts sandy soil (dry), add water to the squeeze-ball state, and compact into place within an hour. Uses: floors and paths (4–6 in on compacted base, kept damp for a week), stabilized rammed earth, CEBs, well aprons and drainage channels where cement is scarce. Clayey soils stabilize better with lime (6–10%) than cement. <b>Earth floors</b>: lay 2–4 in of sandy clay soil with a little chopped straw in thin compacted layers over gravel, let dry, then seal with several coats of linseed oil (traditional) to resist wear and water.</p>')
    return "\n".join(h)
